// v0.7.235 — Paraphrased Bible quotations for AI semantic matching.
//
// Why a SEPARATE file from popular-verses.ts?
//   popular-verses.ts holds the canonical KJV text of each verse —
//   what we'd quote in a printed concordance. This file holds the
//   short, modern, conversational paraphrases an operator (or
//   preacher) actually SAYS during a service:
//
//     KJV  : "Yea, though I walk through the valley of the shadow
//            of death..."
//     Said : "Though I walk through the valley."
//
//   Both forms map to Psalms 23:4. Embedding both — as two separate
//   vectors with the same canonical reference — gives the cosine
//   matcher far better recall on short (2-4 word) spoken phrases.
//   The runtime cache builder in semantic-matcher.ts folds this set
//   in alongside POPULAR_VERSES_KJV and the preacher-phrase catalogue
//   (same v0.7.67 pattern), so a hit on EITHER surface form returns
//   the same canonical reference and the chip/dedupe layer already
//   collapses on `reference` at display time.
//
// Sourcing:
//   Curated from operator-supplied "100 starter paraphrased Bible
//   quotations" reference document (Old + New Testament, the most
//   commonly preached / quoted lines). Many entries deliberately
//   duplicate references already in POPULAR_VERSES_KJV — that is
//   the point: more surface forms per reference = more cosine
//   recall = fewer missed live detections.
//
// Format:
//   Same PopularVerse shape so the cache builder treats every
//   embedding row identically. Book names normalised to the same
//   canonical strings popular-verses.ts uses (`Psalms`, not `Psalm`;
//   `1 Samuel`, not `I Samuel`) so re-fetching the same reference
//   in any translation goes through the same code path.

